[Event "Endgame Classroom"]
[Module "module-1"]
[LessonId "m1-0001"]
[Title "King & Pawn vs King — Opposition"]
[Elo "800"]
[Theme "king and pawn, opposition"]
[Objective "Use the opposition to escort the pawn to promotion."]
[Intro "The most fundamental endgame: King and Pawn versus King. The key concept is the opposition — when two kings face each other with one square between them, the player who does NOT have to move holds the opposition and controls the critical squares."]
[FinalReflection "You held the opposition and promoted the pawn. This pattern decides thousands of games. Remember: the king must lead the pawn, not follow it."]
[MastersNote "The king must be in front of the pawn by at least one square to win."]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "4k3/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[SideToMove "white"]
[Mode "lecture"]

1. Ke7 {Advance the king in front of the pawn. This seizes the opposition and prevents the black king from blocking.|The white king must lead. Playing e6 now would stalemate on the wrong square.} Ke8 {Black's only move — the king is forced back.} 2. e6 {Now the pawn follows with the king already in front.|Classic technique: king leads, pawn follows.} Kd8 {Black steps aside.} 3. e7+ {Check — the pawn advances with tempo.|The pawn advances protected by its king.} Ke8 4. Ke6 {Wait — this is NOT zugzwang. Black must move.|Hold the position. Black is in zugzwang.} Kf7 5. e8=Q+ {Promotion! The queen is born.|A new queen arrives. The endgame principle is complete.} *

[Event "Endgame Classroom"]
[Module "module-1"]
[LessonId "m1-0002"]
[Title "Rook Endgame — Lucena Position"]
[Elo "900"]
[Theme "rook endgame, Lucena, bridge building"]
[Objective "Win using the bridge-building technique to promote the pawn."]
[Intro "The Lucena Position is the cornerstone of rook endgames. You have a pawn on the 7th rank with your king in front. The winning method is called 'building a bridge' — using your rook to shield the king from checks."]
[FinalReflection "You built the bridge perfectly. The Lucena Position appears in nearly every rook endgame — this technique alone is worth 100 rating points."]
[MastersNote "The bridge is built in four rook moves. Know this pattern cold."]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "1K1k4/1P6/8/8/8/8/r7/4R3 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[SideToMove "white"]
[Mode "lecture"]

1. Re4 {Step one of the bridge — bring the rook to the 4th rank to shelter the king.|The rook moves to prepare a shield. This is the first step of the Lucena bridge.} Ra1 {Black keeps checking — the only defence.} 2. Kc7 {The king steps out of the pawn's shadow.|The king escapes the back rank.} Rc1+ 3. Kb6 {Keep going — get the king away from the pawn's queening square.|March the king toward safety.} Rb1+ 4. Kc5 {Approaching the shelter zone.|The king crosses to safety.} Rc1+ 5. Rc4 {The bridge is complete! The rook cuts off the black king's checks.|Bridge built. The king is now sheltered.} *

[Event "Endgame Classroom"]
[Module "module-1"]
[LessonId "m1-0003"]
[Title "Queen vs Rook — Philidor's Method"]
[Elo "1000"]
[Theme "queen vs rook, Philidor, technique"]
[Objective "Winning queen vs rook requires a specific technique to avoid stalemate."]
[Intro "Queen versus rook is a theoretical win, but it requires precise technique. The main danger is stalemate. Philidor's method uses the queen to cut off the defending king and force a fork or skewer."]
[FinalReflection "Excellent technique. Queen vs rook is rarely seen but crucial to know — it can arise from promotions in rook endgames."]
[MastersNote "Never rush. Stalemate traps are everywhere."]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/8/1k6/1r6/1K6 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[SideToMove "white"]
[Mode "puzzle"]

1. Qa4+ {Drive the black king toward the edge.|Check — force the king toward the corner.} Kc3 {Black tries to escape.} 2. Qa3+ {Keep the king on the run.|Another check tightening the net.} Kc4 {The king runs.} 3. Qc1+ {Cut the king off from the centre.|Centralise the queen's attack.} Kb4 {Retreating further.} 4. Qb2 {Pin the rook! Now white threatens to take it.|A silent move that pins the rook to the king.} *

[Event "Endgame Classroom"]
[Module "module-1"]
[LessonId "m1-0004"]
[Title "Rook vs Pawn — Philidor's Draw"]
[Elo "850"]
[Theme "rook endgame, Philidor, drawing technique"]
[Objective "Hold the draw with a rook against a passed pawn using the Philidor method."]
[Intro "When you have a rook and your opponent has a passed pawn about to promote, the drawing technique is to place your rook on the 6th rank (the Philidor position) — not behind the pawn. This cuts off the enemy king and allows you to switch to checking from behind once the pawn advances."]
[FinalReflection "You held the Philidor draw. This technique saves countless endgames. The key idea: rook on the 6th holds, rook behind the pawn draws if timed correctly."]
[MastersNote "Rook on the 6th rank — not the 7th, not the 5th. The 6th."]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "4k3/8/8/3pR3/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[SideToMove "white"]
[Mode "lecture"]

1. Rd5 {Rook to the 6th — cut off the enemy king from helping the pawn.|Place the rook on the 6th rank. This is the Philidor position.} d4 {The pawn advances.} 2. Rd6 {Maintain the barrier — rook stays on the 6th.|Keep cutting off the king.} d3 {Pawn pushes further.} 3. Kd2 {Now centralise the king to help hold the draw.|The king must be active in pawn endgames and rook endgames alike.} Ke7 {Black brings the king.} 4. Rd7+ {Drive the black king back with a check.|Check — push the king away.} Ke6 5. Rd6+ {Repeat the check — the Philidor repetition.|The draw is near.} *

[Event "Endgame Classroom"]
[Module "module-1"]
[LessonId "m1-0005"]
[Title "Knight & King — Coordination"]
[Elo "950"]
[Theme "knight endgame, king activity, coordination"]
[Objective "Use king and knight together to create a mating net."]
[Intro "Knights are slow but deadly in coordination with the king. A common mistake is using the knight alone — the king must participate. In this position, use both pieces to drive the black king to the corner where a forced checkmate awaits."]
[FinalReflection "Perfect coordination. Remember: knights need time to re-route. Plan the knight's path two or three moves ahead. The king-knight tandem is one of the most satisfying forces in chess."]
[MastersNote "Never let the knight sit idle. Every tempo matters with knights."]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/8/4k3/8/4KN2 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[SideToMove "white"]
[Mode "puzzle"]

1. Ke2 {Centralise! The king must join the attack immediately.|The king marches toward the centre to support the knight.} Ke4 {Black centralises too.} 2. Nd2+ {Fork the black king away from the centre.|The knight forks — black must move.} Kd4 {Retreating.} 3. Nf3+ {Check again — keep the king moving.|The knight drives the king.} Kc3 {Black goes to the flank.} 4. Ke3 {King supports the knight.|Close the net with the king.} Kb4 {The king retreats.} 5. Kd4 {Cutting off the king.|The king takes central control.} Ka5 {Black flees to the edge.} 6. Kc5 {Tighten the net.|The king continues to pursue.} Ka4 {Cornered.} 7. Nd4 {The knight hops in to seal the position.|Knight leaps to a key square.} *
